const lessons=[{"id":"communication","title":"Conversations that build trust","cat":"skills","time":8,"image":"editorial-communication","tag":"Communication","description":"Listen with intention. Explain with clarity. Make every interaction matter.","steps":[["Start with the person","Introduce yourself and your role. Ask what matters most today, then listen without interrupting. Acknowledge the emotion before moving to the clinical agenda."],["Make understanding a shared responsibility","Use plain language and small chunks of information. Ask: “To check that I explained this clearly, how will you describe the plan in your own words?” Re-explain and check again when needed."],["Close the loop","Agree on the next step, who owns it, and when it will happen. Explain follow-up and locally appropriate safety-net advice. Use a qualified interpreter when needed rather than assuming understanding."]],"takeaway":"Teach-back is a check on how clearly you explained—not a test of the patient.","question":"A fictional patient has nodded through a discharge explanation. Time is limited. What most directly checks how clearly you explained the next step?","answers":["Repeat the key points, then ask whether any questions remain.","Invite the patient to explain the next step in their own words.","Give a written summary, then ask them to read it after discharge."],"correct":1,"feedback":"An open teach-back prompt reveals what needs clarification without blaming the patient.","reflect":"What is one phrase you could replace with clearer, more human language in your next conversation?","refs":["share","team","research-teachback"],"rationales":["Repeating information can help, but does not itself reveal understanding.","An open teach-back prompt reveals what needs clarification without blaming the patient.","Written support is useful, but does not confirm understanding before the conversation ends."],"evidence":"teachback","visualAlt":"Practitioner listening as an older adult explains their understanding."},{"id":"safety","title":"Small checks. Safer care.","cat":"knowledge","time":10,"image":"topic-safety","tag":"Patient safety","description":"Build safer habits around medications, transitions, and everyday clinical decisions.","steps":[["Pause before acting","Use the identifiers required by your organization. Check allergy information and the intended task against the appropriate record; location or familiarity is not a substitute for identification."],["Reconcile at transitions","Compare the best available medication history with the current plan. Clarify unexplained differences with the responsible prescriber or pharmacist; do not guess. Involve the patient or caregiver."],["Make uncertainty visible","If an order, dose, route, or product is unclear, pause and clarify through your local process before proceeding. Report near misses through the organizational system so the team can learn."]],"takeaway":"A pause to resolve uncertainty is a safety action, not a delay to apologize for.","question":"A fictional transfer record differs from the patient’s medication account. Both could be incomplete. What best addresses the uncertainty?","answers":["Use the latest record while adding the patient’s account as a note.","Ask the patient to confirm which version they followed most recently.","Reconcile the difference with the responsible clinician or pharmacist."],"correct":2,"feedback":"Reconciliation resolves the discrepancy through the appropriate responsible team.","reflect":"Where in your workflow would a deliberate pause prevent a common near miss?","refs":["med","research-medication"],"rationales":["A later timestamp does not resolve an unexplained discrepancy.","The patient’s account matters, but verification with the responsible team is still needed.","Reconciliation resolves the discrepancy through the appropriate responsible team."],"evidence":"medication"},{"id":"empathy","title":"Empathy in everyday practice","cat":"attitudes","time":6,"image":"topic-empathy","tag":"Person-centered care","description":"Bring curiosity, respect, and compassion to the moments that need them most.","steps":[["Notice without assuming","Observe words, tone, and hesitation. Ask “What concerns you most?” rather than inferring feelings from appearance, age, culture, or diagnosis."],["Acknowledge the experience","Try “That sounds like a lot to manage.” Allow silence. Validation does not require agreeing with every request or making promises outside your role."],["Invite partnership","Explore the person’s priorities, practical barriers, and preferred involvement in decisions. Share options honestly, including uncertainty, and agree on an achievable next step."]],"takeaway":"Empathy is curiosity put into words and followed by action.","question":"A fictional patient says the plan is difficult. What is the most useful next response before offering a solution?","answers":["Ask which part is difficult and what is getting in the way.","Offer the simplest available version of the current plan.","Explain why following the plan matters for their recovery."],"correct":0,"feedback":"Understanding the barrier allows a more useful shared response.","reflect":"Think of a time you made an assumption. What curious question could you ask instead? Do not include identifying details.","refs":["share","research-empathy"],"rationales":["Understanding the barrier allows a more useful shared response.","Simplification may help, but may miss the actual barrier.","Explaining importance may help later; first understand what is making the plan difficult."],"evidence":"empathy"},{"id":"sbar","title":"A handover that gets heard","cat":"skills","time":7,"image":"topic-sbar","tag":"Team communication","description":"Use SBAR and closed-loop communication to make the next step unmistakable.","steps":[["Lead with situation and urgency","Identify yourself, your role, the patient using approved identifiers, and the immediate concern. State urgency clearly. Escalate emergencies immediately through local procedures."],["Give relevant background and assessment","Keep background concise. Share recent changes, relevant context, and your assessment within your scope. Separate observed facts from interpretation."],["Make a clear recommendation","Ask explicitly for the review or action you need and by when. Confirm the recipient’s understanding, ownership, and escalation plan if help does not arrive."]],"takeaway":"A good handover transfers responsibility, not just information.","question":"Your fictional SBAR message has been acknowledged. Which closing response best establishes a shared plan?","answers":["Document that the concern was communicated to the receiving team.","Confirm the owner, agreed timing, and contingency if the plan fails.","Repeat the key findings to make sure the message was heard."],"correct":1,"feedback":"Ownership, timing, and contingency make the shared plan actionable.","reflect":"Which part of your usual handover is least explicit: urgency, assessment, ownership, or next step?","refs":["team","research-handoff"],"rationales":["Documentation matters, but does not establish what the receiving team will do.","Ownership, timing, and contingency make the shared plan actionable.","Repeating findings checks part of the message but can leave the action unclear."],"evidence":"handoff"},{"id":"infection","title":"Infection prevention, every time","cat":"knowledge","time":9,"image":"topic-infection","tag":"Infection prevention","description":"Turn standard precautions into consistent, situation-aware habits.","steps":[["Apply standard precautions to everyone","Do not wait for an infection diagnosis. Match hand hygiene, PPE, injection safety, and equipment handling to the task and anticipated exposure."],["Choose protection for the exposure","Assess potential contact with blood, body fluids, mucosa, and nonintact skin. Add transmission-based precautions when indicated by local policy and current organism-specific guidance."],["Protect the next interaction","Perform hand hygiene at required moments; gloves do not replace it. Remove PPE safely and clean or appropriately reprocess shared equipment before the next patient."]],"takeaway":"Standard precautions are the starting point for every patient—not a label reserved for known infections.","question":"Shared equipment looks clean and the next fictional patient has no known infection. What determines preparation for reuse?","answers":["Whether the prior patient was under additional precautions.","Whether the equipment contacted visibly contaminated material.","The approved cleaning or reprocessing process for that equipment."],"correct":2,"feedback":"Use the approved process and instructions; standard precautions apply to all patient care.","reflect":"Which frequently touched piece of shared equipment needs a clearer cleaning handoff in your workplace?","refs":["ipc","research-ipc"],"rationales":["Additional precautions can change requirements but are not the only reason to prepare equipment.","Visible contamination is not the only trigger for an approved reuse process.","Use the approved process and instructions; standard precautions apply to all patient care."],"evidence":"ipc"},{"id":"reasoning","title":"Think clearly under uncertainty","cat":"knowledge","time":8,"image":"topic-reasoning","tag":"Clinical reasoning","description":"Recognize assumptions, seek disconfirming evidence, and ask for a second view.","steps":[["Separate observations from interpretation","State what you know, how reliable it is, and what is missing. Do not let an early label replace ongoing assessment."],["Take a diagnostic pause","Ask: “What else could explain this? What does not fit? What important alternative should I not miss?” Use current, locally approved pathways and decision support within your scope."],["Reassess and safety-net","Discuss uncertainty and the follow-up plan with the responsible team and patient. Specify what change warrants review and who will act on pending results. Seek senior help when concern exceeds your role or confidence."]],"takeaway":"Confidence is useful; a clear plan for uncertainty is safer.","question":"New information conflicts with the initial explanation in a fictional case. What best supports a safe reassessment?","answers":["Name the mismatch and discuss alternatives with the responsible team.","Collect more observations before revisiting the explanation with anyone.","Ask a colleague whether the original explanation remains the most likely."],"correct":0,"feedback":"Making the mismatch explicit helps the team reconsider rather than anchor on an early explanation.","reflect":"What question can you add to your next case discussion to challenge an early assumption?","refs":["team","research-harm"],"editorial":true,"rationales":["Making the mismatch explicit helps the team reconsider rather than anchor on an early explanation.","More information can help, but should not delay communicating a significant concern.","A second opinion helps, but the question should actively invite alternatives and address the mismatch."],"evidence":"harm"},{"id":"speakup","title":"Speak up for safer teams","cat":"attitudes","time":6,"image":"topic-speakup","tag":"Psychological safety","description":"Raise concerns with clarity—and make it easier for others to do the same.","steps":[["Name the concern respectfully","Use observable facts and patient-focused language. TeamSTEPPS CUS can structure a concern: “I am concerned. I am uncomfortable. This is a safety issue.”"],["Persist through the appropriate route","If your concern is not acknowledged, repeat it clearly and escalate according to your local policy. In an emergency, activate the appropriate response without delaying for a perfect script."],["Invite and thank the next voice","Leaders and colleagues can ask, “What are we missing?” Thank people who raise concerns. Debrief systems and decisions without humiliation or blame."]],"takeaway":"Speaking up is a team skill. Receiving a concern well is the other half.","question":"A junior colleague raises a concern during a busy fictional handover. What response best supports speaking up?","answers":["Ask them to write down the concern for the next team meeting.","Thank them, ask what they observed, and check the concern now.","Explain the reasoning behind the current plan before discussing changes."],"correct":1,"feedback":"Acknowledgment and a timely check help the team understand and act on the concern.","reflect":"How will you respond the next time someone questions your plan?","refs":["team","research-handoff"],"rationales":["An active safety concern should be assessed rather than routinely deferred.","Acknowledgment and a timely check help the team understand and act on the concern.","Context can help, but explaining first may close down the concern before it is understood."],"evidence":"handoff"},{"id":"shared","title":"Decisions made together","cat":"skills","time":8,"image":"topic-shared","tag":"Shared decisions","description":"Connect evidence and options with what matters to the individual.","steps":[["Make the choice visible","Invite participation and explain that there are options when a genuine choice exists. Ask how the patient wants to be involved and what matters to them."],["Compare benefits, harms, and uncertainty","Use balanced, plain language and reliable decision aids. When communicating probabilities, use consistent denominators and time frames; avoid presenting relative benefit alone."],["Agree, document, and revisit","Check understanding, explore preferences, and agree on a plan with the responsible clinician. Document the discussion and arrange a way to review the decision if circumstances change."]],"takeaway":"The best available evidence and the person’s priorities belong in the same conversation.","question":"Two reasonable options have different burdens for a fictional patient. What should shape the next conversation?","answers":["Which option has the simplest instructions for the team to explain.","Which option most patients with similar circumstances have chosen.","The options’ trade-offs alongside the person’s priorities and needs."],"correct":2,"feedback":"Balanced evidence, uncertainty, and personal priorities belong in the same discussion.","reflect":"What could you ask to uncover a priority that is not visible in the clinical record?","refs":["share","research-engagement"],"rationales":["Simplicity matters but is only one part of a balanced discussion.","Others’ choices cannot establish this person’s values or needs.","Balanced evidence, uncertainty, and personal priorities belong in the same discussion."],"evidence":"engagement"},{"id":"ai","title":"AI literacy, clinical responsibility","cat":"knowledge","time":9,"image":"topic-ai","tag":"Digital practice","description":"Use emerging tools thoughtfully. Verify outputs. Protect trust and privacy.","steps":[["Know the tool and its limits","Check intended use, organizational approval, and whether it has been evaluated for your setting. A fluent answer can still be wrong, incomplete, or biased."],["Protect sensitive information","Do not enter patient-identifiable or confidential information into unapproved tools. Follow organizational privacy, consent, security, and data-governance requirements."],["Verify before relying","Independently check clinical claims and references using authoritative, current sources. Keep qualified human oversight and follow your organization’s disclosure and documentation policies."]],"takeaway":"A convincing output is not verified evidence. Accountability does not transfer to the tool.","question":"An AI-generated summary includes a plausible claim with an unfamiliar citation. What is the strongest verification step?","answers":["Locate the actual source and check whether it supports the claim.","Ask the tool to provide a more detailed explanation of its citation.","Compare the claim with a second answer from the same tool."],"correct":0,"feedback":"Independent source checking tests both the reference and the claim.","reflect":"What is one clear boundary you need before using a digital or AI tool in your work?","refs":["ai","research-digital"],"rationales":["Independent source checking tests both the reference and the claim.","More detail from the same system can repeat or elaborate an error.","Agreement between generated answers is not independent verification."],"evidence":"digital"},{"id":"wellbeing","title":"Care for the person providing care","cat":"attitudes","time":5,"image":"topic-wellbeing","tag":"Sustainable practice","description":"Notice strain, protect recovery, and build a practical support plan.","steps":[["Notice the load without judgment","Recognize changes in concentration, sleep, mood, or connection. Fatigue is not a character flaw; it can be a signal that workload and recovery need attention."],["Make one feasible adjustment","Where possible, take a brief pause, meet a basic need, or ask a colleague for support. Individual habits do not replace safe staffing, rest, and organizational responsibility."],["Use support early","Discuss workload and safety concerns with appropriate supervisors or occupational health. If distress persists or affects functioning, seek qualified help. For immediate danger, use local emergency support."]],"takeaway":"Sustainable care needs both personal support and working conditions that make safe practice possible.","question":"A fictional colleague reports persistent fatigue affecting concentration. What response addresses both the immediate and continuing concern?","answers":["Suggest a quiet break and review how they feel at the next shift.","Offer immediate support and discuss workload through the proper route.","Share self-care resources and ask them to choose one to practise."],"correct":1,"feedback":"Immediate support and attention to working conditions address more than individual coping.","reflect":"Who can you approach for practical support, and what specific request could you make?","refs":["research-wellbeing"],"editorial":true,"rationales":["A break may help now, but waiting can leave ongoing safety and workload concerns unaddressed.","Immediate support and attention to working conditions address more than individual coping.","Resources can help, but should not replace practical support and organizational responsibility."],"evidence":"wellbeing"},{"id":"equity","title":"Curiosity over assumptions","cat":"attitudes","time":7,"image":"topic-equity","tag":"Equity & cultural humility","description":"Recognize barriers and make room for every patient’s voice.","steps":[["Ask, do not presume","Ask about preferred name, language, communication needs, and what would make care more accessible. Avoid assumptions based on identity or background."],["Make understanding accessible","Offer qualified interpretation and accessible formats through your service. Speak directly to the patient. Ask about hearing, vision, literacy, and practical needs sensitively."],["Check the real-world plan","Explore cost, transport, support, and other barriers. Invite the patient’s preferences and work with the relevant team to adapt the plan while preserving safe care."]],"takeaway":"Equity starts with noticing who a standard approach might leave out.","question":"A fictional patient agrees to a plan, but language and access needs have not been explored. What is the best next step?","answers":["Offer the standard leaflet and invite the family to explain it.","Ask whether the patient is comfortable continuing in this language.","Ask about preferred communication support and practical access needs."],"correct":2,"feedback":"A direct, respectful needs check supports an accessible and workable plan.","reflect":"What is one barrier your standard appointment process may create, and who can help change it?","refs":["share","research-access"],"rationales":["A standard leaflet and family explanation may not meet the person’s communication needs.","This opens the discussion but can still miss format, interpretation, and practical barriers.","A direct, respectful needs check supports an accessible and workable plan."],"evidence":"access"},{"id":"improvement","title":"Turn reflection into better systems","cat":"skills","time":8,"image":"topic-improvement","tag":"Quality improvement","description":"Learn from near misses and test small, measurable changes.","steps":[["Describe the process, not a person to blame","Identify where the workflow becomes fragile. Involve the people who do the work and the patients affected. Use the approved incident process for safety events."],["Choose a small, safe test","Define a specific aim, a measure, and a limited change to try with the appropriate approval. Check for unintended effects as well as intended benefits."],["Review and adapt","Compare what happened with what you expected. Decide with the team whether to adapt, repeat, or stop the test. Share learning without identifiable patient details."]],"takeaway":"A useful reflection ends with a small action, an owner, and a way to learn whether it helped.","question":"A fictional team wants to reduce missing task owners at handover. Which next step supports learning from a small test?","answers":["Agree a limited change, a measure, and a check for added burden.","Introduce an ownership field across the service and gather comments.","Repeat the handover training and count how many staff attend."],"correct":0,"feedback":"A defined test, measure, and balancing check make the result interpretable.","reflect":"What small workflow improvement could your team test, and how would you know whether it helped?","refs":["team","research-audit"],"editorial":true,"rationales":["A defined test, measure, and balancing check make the result interpretable.","A wider change can create avoidable disruption before a small test has generated learning.","Attendance is an activity measure; it does not show whether missing ownership improved."],"evidence":"audit"},{"id":"health-literacy","cat":"knowledge","title":"Health literacy is a shared responsibility","tag":"Accessible information","image":"topic-health-literacy","ref":"literacy","evidence":"teachback","tool":"teach","description":"Reduce jargon, information overload and the hidden effort of navigating care.","steps":[["Design for everyone","Understanding can change with stress, unfamiliar language and a new setting. Use clear information for everyone rather than trying to identify who appears to have low literacy."],["Make the next action visible","Prioritise what the person needs to do next. Use everyday words, a short written summary and accessible formats. Ask about language and communication preferences."],["Check your explanation","Use an open teach-back invitation, listen for gaps, rephrase and check again. Ask whether the plan can work with the person’s practical circumstances."]],"outcome":"Recognise an information barrier and redesign one explanation.","takeaway":"An explanation succeeds when the person can use it.","reflect":"Which instruction in your service needs a clearer next action?","example":["A fictional appointment message lists several unfamiliar abbreviations.","The instructions are standard; please read them again.","Let us put this into everyday words and identify your next step together.","Ask a person to explain the next step after one short explanation."],"checks":[["A person follows a conversation until new abbreviations appear. What helps?","Replace abbreviations, explain one action and check understanding.","Repeat the abbreviations more slowly.","Assume agreement means understanding."],["What should guide an accessible format?","The person’s communication needs and preferences.","The cheapest format in every case.","How confident the person appears."],["A clear explanation still produces an impractical plan. What next?","Explore practical barriers and involve the appropriate team.","Give the same explanation again.","Label the person unmotivated."]],"time":8,"refs":["literacy","research-teachback"],"editorial":true,"question":"A person follows a conversation until new abbreviations appear. What helps?","answers":["Repeat the abbreviations more slowly.","Replace abbreviations, explain one action and check understanding.","Assume agreement means understanding."],"correct":1,"rationales":["This leaves the key issue unresolved. Understanding can change with stress, unfamiliar language and a new setting. Use clear information for everyone rather than trying to identify who appears to have low literacy.","This applies the principle: Understanding can change with stress, unfamiliar language and a new setting. Use clear information for everyone rather than trying to identify who appears to have low literacy.","This leaves the key issue unresolved. Understanding can change with stress, unfamiliar language and a new setting. Use clear information for everyone rather than trying to identify who appears to have low literacy."],"feedback":"This applies the principle: Understanding can change with stress, unfamiliar language and a new setting. Use clear information for everyone rather than trying to identify who appears to have low literacy."},{"id":"risk-evidence","cat":"knowledge","title":"Make numbers mean something","tag":"Risk & evidence","image":"topic-risk-evidence","ref":"share","evidence":"engagement","tool":"evidence","description":"Read absolute risk, relative change and uncertainty without being misled.","steps":[["Start with the denominator","Ask how many people, over what time, and with which outcome. Compare the same population, denominator and period. A count without a denominator is hard to interpret."],["Show both sides of a change","In a fictional example, an outcome falls from 4 in 100 to 2 in 100. That is 2 percentage points less, and a 50% relative reduction. These describe the same change in different ways."],["Check transferability","Examine the study setting, design and uncertainty. An association is not automatically a causal effect, and a result from one setting is not a forecast for another."]],"outcome":"Explain a risk change using an absolute difference and a consistent denominator.","takeaway":"Always ask: compared with what, among whom, and over how long?","reflect":"Where could a percentage in your work benefit from a denominator?","example":["A fictional flyer says an intervention halves an outcome.","A 50% reduction means 50 fewer people in every 100.","What were the starting and ending risks, time period and population?","Check the absolute figures behind one relative-risk claim."],"checks":[["A fictional risk changes from 4% to 2%. What is the absolute reduction?","2 percentage points.","50 percentage points.","2% of the original 4%."],["Two rates use different follow-up periods. What is needed?","A comparable time frame before interpreting the difference.","The larger percentage alone.","A more impressive chart."],["A before-and-after study shows improvement. What is justified?","An observed association, with design limitations considered.","Proof that the change caused every improvement.","A guarantee of the same effect locally."]],"time":8,"refs":["share","research-engagement"],"editorial":true,"question":"A fictional risk changes from 4% to 2%. What is the absolute reduction?","answers":["50 percentage points.","2 percentage points.","2% of the original 4%."],"correct":1,"rationales":["This leaves the key issue unresolved. Ask how many people, over what time, and with which outcome. Compare the same population, denominator and period. A count without a denominator is hard to interpret.","This applies the principle: Ask how many people, over what time, and with which outcome. Compare the same population, denominator and period. A count without a denominator is hard to interpret.","This leaves the key issue unresolved. Ask how many people, over what time, and with which outcome. Compare the same population, denominator and period. A count without a denominator is hard to interpret."],"feedback":"This applies the principle: Ask how many people, over what time, and with which outcome. Compare the same population, denominator and period. A count without a denominator is hard to interpret."},{"id":"human-factors","cat":"knowledge","title":"Design around human limits","tag":"Human factors","image":"topic-human-factors","ref":"patient","evidence":"interruptions","tool":"qi","description":"Recognise interruptions, memory load and fragile systems before blaming people.","steps":[["Look at work as it happens","Map the actual task, tools, environment, handoffs and competing demands. Ask the people doing the work where the process becomes difficult."],["Build a reliable support","Reduce unnecessary complexity. Use a visible task owner, a clear cue or an approved checklist at the point of work. A reminder to concentrate is a weak response to a recurring design problem."],["Test for new burden","Try a limited approved change and check its effect on errors, time and workarounds. Include people who might experience new barriers."]],"outcome":"Identify a system condition and propose a small testable safeguard.","takeaway":"A reliable process supports people when attention and memory are stretched.","reflect":"Which recurring interruption deserves a system change?","example":["A fictional team misses a task after repeated interruptions.","Everyone needs to remember better.","Let us map the interruption and test a visible task cue with a named owner.","Map one fragile step with the people who perform it."],"checks":[["A recurring omission survives repeated reminders. What is useful?","Study the workflow and test a support at the fragile step.","Send a stronger reminder.","Identify the least experienced person."],["What is a balancing measure for a new checklist?","Added time or duplication caused by the checklist.","Only the number of printed copies.","Only attendance at the launch."],["Why involve people doing the work?","They can reveal constraints absent from the written procedure.","They can guarantee the solution will work.","They replace the need for approval."]],"time":8,"refs":["patient","research-interruptions"],"editorial":true,"question":"A recurring omission survives repeated reminders. What is useful?","answers":["Send a stronger reminder.","Study the workflow and test a support at the fragile step.","Identify the least experienced person."],"correct":1,"rationales":["This leaves the key issue unresolved. Map the actual task, tools, environment, handoffs and competing demands. Ask the people doing the work where the process becomes difficult.","This applies the principle: Map the actual task, tools, environment, handoffs and competing demands. Ask the people doing the work where the process becomes difficult.","This leaves the key issue unresolved. Map the actual task, tools, environment, handoffs and competing demands. Ask the people doing the work where the process becomes difficult."],"feedback":"This applies the principle: Map the actual task, tools, environment, handoffs and competing demands. Ask the people doing the work where the process becomes difficult."},{"id":"med-transitions","cat":"knowledge","title":"Medication lists across transitions","tag":"Medication safety","image":"topic-med-transitions","ref":"med","evidence":"medication","tool":"safety","description":"Recognise discrepancies and clarify the intended plan with the responsible team.","steps":[["Compare credible accounts","Use the approved reconciliation process to compare available records and the person’s account. Identify an unexplained omission, duplication or change."],["Clarify, do not prescribe by inference","Record the uncertainty and route it to the responsible clinician or pharmacist. Do not decide to start, stop or change a medicine from this learning exercise."],["Communicate the verified plan","Ensure the agreed resolution reaches the authorised record, receiving team and person through the approved workflow. Confirm ownership of unresolved items."]],"outcome":"Distinguish a discrepancy from a verified medication decision.","takeaway":"A discrepancy is a question to resolve, not an instruction to guess.","reflect":"Who owns unresolved medication questions at your handover?","example":["Two fictional lists disagree about whether Medicine A is current.","The newest list must be right.","The accounts conflict. I will seek verification through the responsible clinician or pharmacist.","Clarify ownership of a reconciliation question."],"checks":[["A medicine appears on only one transfer list. What can you conclude?","There is a discrepancy requiring appropriate verification.","The medicine should be stopped.","The older record is wrong."],["Which person’s account is relevant?","The patient or caregiver, alongside approved reliable sources.","Only the newest computer entry.","Only the receiving team’s assumptions."],["Verification resolves a discrepancy. What follows?","Communicate and document the agreed plan through the approved process.","Keep the answer in memory.","Delete the uncertainty without communicating."]],"time":8,"refs":["med","research-medication"],"editorial":true,"question":"A medicine appears on only one transfer list. What can you conclude?","answers":["The medicine should be stopped.","There is a discrepancy requiring appropriate verification.","The older record is wrong."],"correct":1,"rationales":["This leaves the key issue unresolved. Use the approved reconciliation process to compare available records and the person’s account. Identify an unexplained omission, duplication or change.","This applies the principle: Use the approved reconciliation process to compare available records and the person’s account. Identify an unexplained omission, duplication or change.","This leaves the key issue unresolved. Use the approved reconciliation process to compare available records and the person’s account. Identify an unexplained omission, duplication or change."],"feedback":"This applies the principle: Use the approved reconciliation process to compare available records and the person’s account. Identify an unexplained omission, duplication or change."},{"id":"deterioration","cat":"knowledge","title":"Recognise a change. Escalate clearly.","tag":"Recognition & escalation","image":"topic-deterioration","ref":"team","evidence":"handoff","tool":"sbar","description":"Make changes, urgency and responsibility explicit without waiting for certainty.","steps":[["Notice the change","Describe the observed change and relevant context within your role. Do not let a reassuring label replace current observations or the person’s expressed concern."],["Use the immediate local route","For urgent concern, use the local emergency or escalation pathway immediately. A communication mnemonic must not become a waiting sequence or substitute for clinical assessment."],["Keep the loop open until action is clear","Confirm the receiving person, expected response and contingency if help does not arrive. Re-escalate through local policy if the concern remains unresolved."]],"outcome":"Give a clear change-and-concern message with ownership and contingency.","takeaway":"Communicate significant concern before certainty arrives.","reflect":"How do you confirm that a concern has an action owner?","example":["A fictional record says stable but new information is concerning.","The earlier note was reassuring, so I will wait.","There is a new change and I am concerned. I will activate the appropriate local response now.","Review where to find your local escalation pathway."],"checks":[["Urgent concern emerges during a practice handover. What takes priority?","The immediate local escalation pathway within your role.","Finishing a perfect SBAR script.","Waiting for the next routine handover."],["What does a reassuring earlier note establish?","Earlier context; it does not negate a new change.","That reassessment is unnecessary.","That any new concern is incorrect."],["Acknowledgment arrives but no action owner is named. What remains?","Confirm responsibility, response timing and contingency.","Assume the task is assigned.","End the conversation to avoid repetition."]],"time":8,"refs":["team","research-handoff"],"editorial":true,"question":"Urgent concern emerges during a practice handover. What takes priority?","answers":["Finishing a perfect SBAR script.","The immediate local escalation pathway within your role.","Waiting for the next routine handover."],"correct":1,"rationales":["This leaves the key issue unresolved. Describe the observed change and relevant context within your role. Do not let a reassuring label replace current observations or the person’s expressed concern.","This applies the principle: Describe the observed change and relevant context within your role. Do not let a reassuring label replace current observations or the person’s expressed concern.","This leaves the key issue unresolved. Describe the observed change and relevant context within your role. Do not let a reassuring label replace current observations or the person’s expressed concern."],"feedback":"This applies the principle: Describe the observed change and relevant context within your role. Do not let a reassuring label replace current observations or the person’s expressed concern."},{"id":"data-stewardship","cat":"knowledge","title":"Protect information. Preserve trust.","tag":"Digital stewardship","image":"topic-data-stewardship","ref":"ai","evidence":"digital","tool":"evidence","description":"Choose approved channels, minimise disclosure and check digital outputs.","steps":[["Know the purpose and channel","Use organisationally approved systems and authorised access for a defined purpose. Consult current information-governance policy rather than inferring permission from convenience."],["Minimise what is shared","Names are not the only identifiers. Dates, locations, rare details and combinations can identify someone. Removing a name alone does not establish anonymity."],["Verify and report","Independently check material before relying on it. If information is sent to the wrong place, follow the local incident process promptly and preserve relevant facts."]],"outcome":"Recognise an unsafe information-sharing assumption and choose an approved route.","takeaway":"Useful information still needs an authorised purpose and destination.","reflect":"What digital convenience needs a clearer boundary in your work?","example":["A fictional note without a name is proposed for a public tool.","No name means no privacy concern.","Other details may identify the person; we need an approved workflow before sharing.","Identify the approved information-governance contact for your setting."],"checks":[["A note has no name but includes a rare event and location. Is it anonymous?","Not necessarily; combinations of details can identify someone.","Yes, because the name was removed.","Yes, if shared only once."],["A file is sent to the wrong recipient. What helps?","Follow the local reporting and containment process promptly.","Wait to see whether anyone complains.","Assume deletion resolves every consequence."],["A digital output is fluent and well formatted. What does that establish?","Presentation quality; accuracy and suitability still require checks.","Independent verification.","Organisational permission to use it."]],"time":8,"refs":["ai","research-digital"],"editorial":true,"question":"A note has no name but includes a rare event and location. Is it anonymous?","answers":["Yes, because the name was removed.","Not necessarily; combinations of details can identify someone.","Yes, if shared only once."],"correct":1,"rationales":["This leaves the key issue unresolved. Use organisationally approved systems and authorised access for a defined purpose. Consult current information-governance policy rather than inferring permission from convenience.","This applies the principle: Use organisationally approved systems and authorised access for a defined purpose. Consult current information-governance policy rather than inferring permission from convenience.","This leaves the key issue unresolved. Use organisationally approved systems and authorised access for a defined purpose. Consult current information-governance policy rather than inferring permission from convenience."],"feedback":"This applies the principle: Use organisationally approved systems and authorised access for a defined purpose. Consult current information-governance policy rather than inferring permission from convenience."},{"id":"teachback-skill","cat":"skills","title":"Explain. Listen. Repair.","tag":"Teach-back","image":"topic-teachback-skill","ref":"literacy","evidence":"teachback","tool":"teach","description":"Practise a non-judgmental loop that reveals and repairs an explanation gap.","steps":[["Share one manageable idea","Use plain language, then pause. Ask about preferred language, format and interpretation support. Focus on one useful next action."],["Invite the person’s explanation","Say: To check that I explained it clearly, how would you describe what happens next? Frame the check around your explanation."],["Repair and check again","Thank the person, identify the gap and explain it in a different way. Repeat the check until the next step is clear, using appropriate support."]],"outcome":"Construct an explanation-check-repair loop without blame.","takeaway":"Teach-back is a loop, not a yes/no question.","reflect":"Which phrase will you use when the first explanation does not land?","example":["A fictional patient explains a follow-up plan differently.","You were not listening.","Thank you. I need to explain that part more clearly. Let us try it another way.","Rehearse one repair phrase with a colleague."],"checks":[["Which prompt invites teach-back?","How would you describe the next step, so I can check my explanation?","Do you understand everything?","Why did you forget?"],["An explanation gap appears. What next?","Rephrase the unclear part and check again.","Repeat the whole script unchanged.","Record agreement anyway."],["Who is being checked in teach-back?","The clarity of the explanation and shared understanding.","The person’s intelligence.","The person’s willingness to please the team."]],"time":8,"refs":["literacy","research-teachback"],"editorial":true,"question":"Which prompt invites teach-back?","answers":["Do you understand everything?","How would you describe the next step, so I can check my explanation?","Why did you forget?"],"correct":1,"rationales":["This leaves the key issue unresolved. Use plain language, then pause. Ask about preferred language, format and interpretation support. Focus on one useful next action.","This applies the principle: Use plain language, then pause. Ask about preferred language, format and interpretation support. Focus on one useful next action.","This leaves the key issue unresolved. Use plain language, then pause. Ask about preferred language, format and interpretation support. Focus on one useful next action."],"feedback":"This applies the principle: Use plain language, then pause. Ask about preferred language, format and interpretation support. Focus on one useful next action."},{"id":"conflict","cat":"skills","title":"Disagree without losing the purpose","tag":"Constructive disagreement","image":"topic-conflict","ref":"team","evidence":"empathy","tool":"huddle","description":"Move from personal friction to observable concerns and a shared next step.","steps":[["Describe the observable issue","Separate what happened from your interpretation of motives. Use a calm statement about a specific event and its effect."],["Find the shared purpose","Ask for the other perspective and name the common aim. Explore constraints without assuming that disagreement reflects poor intent."],["Agree and follow through","Make a specific request, confirm an owner and arrange follow-up. Use the appropriate support or escalation route for unresolved concerns or unacceptable conduct."]],"outcome":"Turn a personal accusation into an observable concern and a clear request.","takeaway":"A useful disagreement ends with a clearer next action.","reflect":"Which assumption about a colleague could you replace with a question?","example":["A fictional handover lacks a named task owner.","You never take responsibility.","This task has no named owner. Can we agree who will act and when?","Rephrase one accusation as an observation and request."],"checks":[["Which opening keeps a disagreement actionable?","The task has no named owner; can we clarify responsibility?","You never care about the team.","Everyone knows who is at fault."],["A colleague offers a different account. What helps?","Explore their observations and the shared goal.","Dismiss it because your account came first.","Infer their motives."],["A serious concern remains unresolved. What next?","Use the appropriate organisational escalation or support route.","Treat agreement as more important than safety.","Keep repeating a personal accusation."]],"time":8,"refs":["team","research-empathy"],"editorial":true,"question":"Which opening keeps a disagreement actionable?","answers":["You never care about the team.","The task has no named owner; can we clarify responsibility?","Everyone knows who is at fault."],"correct":1,"rationales":["This leaves the key issue unresolved. Separate what happened from your interpretation of motives. Use a calm statement about a specific event and its effect.","This applies the principle: Separate what happened from your interpretation of motives. Use a calm statement about a specific event and its effect.","This leaves the key issue unresolved. Separate what happened from your interpretation of motives. Use a calm statement about a specific event and its effect."],"feedback":"This applies the principle: Separate what happened from your interpretation of motives. Use a calm statement about a specific event and its effect."},{"id":"documentation","cat":"skills","title":"Write so the next person can act","tag":"Documentation","image":"topic-documentation","ref":"team","evidence":"handoff","tool":"sbar","description":"Separate facts, decisions and responsibility in a concise learning note.","steps":[["Record what was observed","Use relevant, accurate observations and the time or source where appropriate. Distinguish a person’s account, your observation and an interpretation."],["Make the plan actionable","Include the agreed action, responsible person, timing and unresolved questions according to your local documentation requirements."],["Check the receiving view","Ask whether another authorised colleague could identify what to do next. Correct errors through the approved record process; documentation does not replace urgent communication."]],"outcome":"Identify missing ownership and uncertainty in a fictional note.","takeaway":"A record should make the next action easier to understand.","reflect":"What does your next colleague need from a note that you sometimes omit?","example":["A fictional note reads: team aware, follow up later.","The team has been told.","I will record the agreed action, named owner, timing and any outstanding question in the approved record.","Check one fictional note for a missing owner or time."],"checks":[["What is missing from team aware, follow up later?","An explicit action owner and timing.","More reassuring wording.","A longer paragraph."],["Which sentence separates observation from interpretation?","I observed a change; I am concerned it needs review.","This must be the only possible explanation.","It is obviously fine."],["Is writing an urgent concern enough?","No; use the appropriate direct escalation as well.","Yes, if the note is long.","Yes, if the record has a timestamp."]],"time":8,"refs":["team","research-handoff"],"editorial":true,"question":"What is missing from team aware, follow up later?","answers":["More reassuring wording.","An explicit action owner and timing.","A longer paragraph."],"correct":1,"rationales":["This leaves the key issue unresolved. Use relevant, accurate observations and the time or source where appropriate. Distinguish a person’s account, your observation and an interpretation.","This applies the principle: Use relevant, accurate observations and the time or source where appropriate. Distinguish a person’s account, your observation and an interpretation.","This leaves the key issue unresolved. Use relevant, accurate observations and the time or source where appropriate. Distinguish a person’s account, your observation and an interpretation."],"feedback":"This applies the principle: Use relevant, accurate observations and the time or source where appropriate. Distinguish a person’s account, your observation and an interpretation."},{"id":"risk-dialogue","cat":"skills","title":"Have a balanced risk conversation","tag":"Shared understanding","image":"topic-risk-dialogue","ref":"share","evidence":"engagement","tool":"teach","description":"Use consistent numbers, honest uncertainty and the person’s priorities.","steps":[["Make the comparison fair","Use the same denominator, time horizon and outcome for benefits and harms. Include the baseline and avoid relative percentages on their own."],["Check what the number means","Invite the person to describe their interpretation. A visual display can support a conversation, but it does not replace accessible explanation."],["Connect to what matters","Explore the person’s preferences, practical burden and need for more information. Involve the responsible clinician and arrange a way to revisit the decision."]],"outcome":"Explain a fictional risk comparison without exaggerating benefit.","takeaway":"Balanced numbers support a decision; they do not choose it for the person.","reflect":"What matters to this person beyond the outcome percentage?","example":["A fictional option reduces an outcome from 10 in 100 to 8 in 100.","It cuts the risk by 20%, so it is clearly best.","In this example that is 2 fewer people in 100 over the same period. Let us compare burdens and uncertainty too.","Practise a same-denominator risk explanation."],"checks":[["A fictional outcome changes from 10/100 to 8/100. Which explanation is balanced?","2 fewer in 100 over the same period, alongside harms and uncertainty.","A 20% change with no baseline.","80 fewer people in 100."],["What should accompany a visual risk chart?","An accessible explanation and a check of interpretation.","A statement that the chart chooses the best option.","A promise of individual benefit."],["A person prioritises a different burden. What next?","Explore that priority with the responsible clinician.","Repeat the percentage until they agree.","Assume they misunderstood because they disagree."]],"time":8,"refs":["share","research-engagement"],"editorial":true,"question":"A fictional outcome changes from 10/100 to 8/100. Which explanation is balanced?","answers":["A 20% change with no baseline.","2 fewer in 100 over the same period, alongside harms and uncertainty.","80 fewer people in 100."],"correct":1,"rationales":["This leaves the key issue unresolved. Use the same denominator, time horizon and outcome for benefits and harms. Include the baseline and avoid relative percentages on their own.","This applies the principle: Use the same denominator, time horizon and outcome for benefits and harms. Include the baseline and avoid relative percentages on their own.","This leaves the key issue unresolved. Use the same denominator, time horizon and outcome for benefits and harms. Include the baseline and avoid relative percentages on their own."],"feedback":"This applies the principle: Use the same denominator, time horizon and outcome for benefits and harms. Include the baseline and avoid relative percentages on their own."},{"id":"feedback","cat":"skills","title":"Feedback that leads somewhere","tag":"Coaching & learning","image":"topic-feedback","ref":"team","evidence":"audit","tool":"reflect","description":"Describe behaviour, invite perspective and agree a small next experiment.","steps":[["Choose a useful moment","Use a respectful setting and clarify the learning purpose. Address urgent safety concerns immediately through the appropriate route rather than saving them for routine feedback."],["Be specific and curious","Describe an observable behaviour and its effect. Ask how the other person saw the situation before offering a conclusion. Avoid labels about character."],["Agree a next step","Choose a manageable behaviour to try, the support needed and a time to review. Feedback becomes useful when it changes what can happen next."]],"outcome":"Write behaviour-focused feedback with an agreed next step.","takeaway":"Useful feedback gives someone something specific they can try.","reflect":"How could your next feedback include an invitation to respond?","example":["In a fictional rehearsal, ownership was omitted at handover.","You are bad at handovers.","The action owner was missing. How did you see that moment, and could we practise an explicit closing request?","Give one specific observation and invite a response."],"checks":[["Which feedback is most actionable?","The owner was unclear; let us practise naming one.","You are careless.","Try to be better."],["Why invite the learner’s perspective?","It can reveal context and shape a useful next step.","It guarantees agreement.","It removes the need to discuss impact."],["What completes a feedback conversation?","An agreed action, support and review point.","A label about personality.","A score without explanation."]],"time":8,"refs":["team","research-audit"],"editorial":true,"question":"Which feedback is most actionable?","answers":["You are careless.","The owner was unclear; let us practise naming one.","Try to be better."],"correct":1,"rationales":["This leaves the key issue unresolved. Use a respectful setting and clarify the learning purpose. Address urgent safety concerns immediately through the appropriate route rather than saving them for routine feedback.","This applies the principle: Use a respectful setting and clarify the learning purpose. Address urgent safety concerns immediately through the appropriate route rather than saving them for routine feedback.","This leaves the key issue unresolved. Use a respectful setting and clarify the learning purpose. Address urgent safety concerns immediately through the appropriate route rather than saving them for routine feedback."],"feedback":"This applies the principle: Use a respectful setting and clarify the learning purpose. Address urgent safety concerns immediately through the appropriate route rather than saving them for routine feedback."},{"id":"debrief","cat":"skills","title":"Turn an event into team learning","tag":"Debriefing","image":"editorial-debrief","ref":"team","evidence":"simulation","tool":"huddle","description":"Compare expectations with events, identify conditions and close the learning loop.","steps":[["Create a respectful purpose","Clarify that the discussion aims to learn and improve. Use the appropriate formal reporting or investigation process where required. Do not include confidential case details in this app."],["Compare plan and reality","Ask what was expected, what happened, what helped and what made the task difficult. Invite different roles and distinguish facts from assumptions."],["Choose a follow-through","Agree one improvement question or action with a named owner and review date. Share learning through approved channels and arrange support when needed."]],"outcome":"Structure a short debrief with one owned improvement action.","takeaway":"A debrief needs both curiosity and follow-through.","reflect":"Which action from a recent learning discussion still needs an owner?","example":["A fictional team finishes a challenging practice session.","We finished, so there is nothing to discuss.","What helped, what was difficult, and which one change will we review next time?","Close a debrief with one named learning action."],"checks":[["Which question opens a useful debrief?","What did we expect, and what actually happened?","Who made us look bad?","How can we prove nothing went wrong?"],["A debrief identifies three ideas but no owner. What is missing?","An agreed action, owner and review point.","More ideas alone.","A longer meeting title."],["Does a learning debrief replace required incident reporting?","No; follow the appropriate formal process as well.","Yes, if everyone attends.","Yes, if the discussion is supportive."]],"time":8,"refs":["team","research-simulation"],"editorial":true,"question":"Which question opens a useful debrief?","answers":["Who made us look bad?","What did we expect, and what actually happened?","How can we prove nothing went wrong?"],"correct":1,"rationales":["This leaves the key issue unresolved. Clarify that the discussion aims to learn and improve. Use the appropriate formal reporting or investigation process where required. Do not include confidential case details in this app.","This applies the principle: Clarify that the discussion aims to learn and improve. Use the appropriate formal reporting or investigation process where required. Do not include confidential case details in this app.","This leaves the key issue unresolved. Clarify that the discussion aims to learn and improve. Use the appropriate formal reporting or investigation process where required. Do not include confidential case details in this app."],"feedback":"This applies the principle: Clarify that the discussion aims to learn and improve. Use the appropriate formal reporting or investigation process where required. Do not include confidential case details in this app.","visualAlt":"Three healthcare colleagues reflecting together on a fictional learning session."},{"id":"humility","cat":"attitudes","title":"Be willing to update your view","tag":"Intellectual humility","image":"topic-humility","ref":"patient","evidence":"harm","tool":"evidence","description":"Hold a working explanation while staying open to information that challenges it.","steps":[["Name what you do not know","Distinguish a working explanation from a verified fact. State uncertainty in useful terms, including the information or review needed."],["Invite a different view","Ask what does not fit and who may have another perspective. A patient, caregiver or colleague may notice something missing from the current account."],["Update and communicate","Revise the explanation when evidence changes. Tell relevant people what changed and what happens next within the appropriate clinical process."]],"outcome":"Make uncertainty explicit and invite disconfirming information.","takeaway":"Changing your mind can be a sign of careful thinking.","reflect":"What would make you reconsider your current explanation?","example":["New information challenges a fictional team’s first view.","We have already decided.","This does not fit our earlier view. What should we reconsider and who needs to review it?","Ask what information would change an assumption."],"checks":[["Which statement shows useful humility?","Here is my current view and the uncertainty we need to resolve.","I cannot be wrong.","No one should question a confident explanation."],["A patient identifies a mismatch. What helps?","Explore it and review the working explanation.","Dismiss it because it was not in the record.","Explain why the first view must be right."],["What should follow a revised explanation?","Communicate the change and next action to relevant people.","Keep the update private.","Erase the learning discussion."]],"time":8,"refs":["patient","research-harm"],"editorial":true,"question":"Which statement shows useful humility?","answers":["I cannot be wrong.","Here is my current view and the uncertainty we need to resolve.","No one should question a confident explanation."],"correct":1,"rationales":["This leaves the key issue unresolved. Distinguish a working explanation from a verified fact. State uncertainty in useful terms, including the information or review needed.","This applies the principle: Distinguish a working explanation from a verified fact. State uncertainty in useful terms, including the information or review needed.","This leaves the key issue unresolved. Distinguish a working explanation from a verified fact. State uncertainty in useful terms, including the information or review needed."],"feedback":"This applies the principle: Distinguish a working explanation from a verified fact. State uncertainty in useful terms, including the information or review needed."},{"id":"dignity","cat":"attitudes","title":"Protect dignity in small moments","tag":"Respect & choice","image":"topic-dignity","ref":"disability","evidence":"access","tool":"teach","description":"Ask permission, speak directly and make participation accessible.","steps":[["Address the person","Use the person’s preferred name and communication approach. Do not talk over someone to their companion or assume preferences from appearance or disability."],["Make choice and privacy visible","Explain your role and the intended conversation or task. Ask permission within the applicable consent process, protect privacy and respect the person’s questions."],["Adapt the interaction","Ask what support would help. Arrange approved interpretation or accessible formats and involve companions according to the person’s preferences and applicable policy."]],"outcome":"Recognise a dignity barrier and offer a respectful alternative.","takeaway":"Respect is expressed through permission, privacy and participation.","reflect":"Which small routine could give a person more voice or privacy?","example":["A fictional discussion addresses a companion while the patient is present.","Tell us what they need.","I would like to hear from you directly. What support would make this discussion easier?","Ask a communication preference before assuming one."],"checks":[["A companion answers every question. What supports dignity?","Address the person directly and ask their preferred involvement.","Assume the companion should decide.","Avoid the person to save time."],["What should guide communication support?","The person’s expressed needs and appropriate service support.","An assumption based on age.","The standard format regardless of need."],["What makes permission meaningful in a conversation?","Explain the purpose and allow questions within the applicable process.","Treat silence as agreement in every setting.","Rely only on the room number."]],"time":8,"refs":["disability","research-access"],"editorial":true,"question":"A companion answers every question. What supports dignity?","answers":["Assume the companion should decide.","Address the person directly and ask their preferred involvement.","Avoid the person to save time."],"correct":1,"rationales":["This leaves the key issue unresolved. Use the person’s preferred name and communication approach. Do not talk over someone to their companion or assume preferences from appearance or disability.","This applies the principle: Use the person’s preferred name and communication approach. Do not talk over someone to their companion or assume preferences from appearance or disability.","This leaves the key issue unresolved. Use the person’s preferred name and communication approach. Do not talk over someone to their companion or assume preferences from appearance or disability."],"feedback":"This applies the principle: Use the person’s preferred name and communication approach. Do not talk over someone to their companion or assume preferences from appearance or disability."},{"id":"just-culture","cat":"attitudes","title":"Learn fairly from an error","tag":"Just culture","image":"topic-just-culture","ref":"patient","evidence":"harm","tool":"qi","description":"Explore system conditions while retaining fair accountability.","steps":[["Begin with facts and immediate safety","Use the appropriate response, reporting and support process. Record what is known and avoid speculation about an individual’s motives."],["Explore the conditions","Ask about workload, tools, interruptions, unclear responsibilities and other influences. A systems approach does not mean ignoring deliberate unsafe behaviour or professional obligations."],["Create a fair learning response","Use the organisation’s established process to evaluate conduct and context fairly. Agree changes, support and follow-up rather than treating a single training reminder as the whole solution."]],"outcome":"Distinguish system learning from blame or the absence of accountability.","takeaway":"Fairness needs both context and accountability.","reflect":"Which system question should come before a judgment about a person?","example":["A fictional team discovers a near miss.","Find the person responsible and close the issue.","Let us establish the facts, follow the reporting process and understand the conditions fairly.","Ask how the process made the error possible."],"checks":[["What does a systems approach require?","Understanding conditions alongside fair accountability.","Ignoring every individual action.","Blaming the newest colleague."],["A near miss caused no harm. What is useful?","Report and learn through the approved process.","Assume the process is safe.","Wait for harm before reviewing."],["Which response supports learning?","Investigate facts and context through an established fair process.","Publicly speculate about motives.","Replace the whole investigation with a reminder."]],"time":8,"refs":["patient","research-harm"],"editorial":true,"question":"What does a systems approach require?","answers":["Ignoring every individual action.","Understanding conditions alongside fair accountability.","Blaming the newest colleague."],"correct":1,"rationales":["This leaves the key issue unresolved. Use the appropriate response, reporting and support process. Record what is known and avoid speculation about an individual’s motives.","This applies the principle: Use the appropriate response, reporting and support process. Record what is known and avoid speculation about an individual’s motives.","This leaves the key issue unresolved. Use the appropriate response, reporting and support process. Record what is known and avoid speculation about an individual’s motives."],"feedback":"This applies the principle: Use the appropriate response, reporting and support process. Record what is known and avoid speculation about an individual’s motives."},{"id":"accountability","cat":"attitudes","title":"Own the next action","tag":"Professional accountability","image":"topic-accountability","ref":"team","evidence":"handoff","tool":"sbar","description":"Make commitments visible, surface limits and follow through.","steps":[["Be clear about responsibility","State what you can do within your role and what needs another person’s authority or expertise. Do not accept a task by implication when responsibility is unclear."],["Make limits explicit","If a commitment cannot be met, communicate the constraint early through the appropriate route. Agree an alternative owner or contingency rather than allowing a silent gap."],["Close the commitment","Confirm the agreed action and record or communicate completion through the approved process. Make outstanding items visible at transition."]],"outcome":"Turn an implied task into a clear commitment or escalation.","takeaway":"Accountability includes saying when a task needs help.","reflect":"Which commitment needs an explicit owner, deadline or contingency?","example":["A fictional colleague assumes someone will arrange a review.","Somebody will handle it.","Let us confirm who owns the action, by when, and what happens if they cannot complete it.","Close one handover with named responsibility."],"checks":[["A task is beyond your authority. What is accountable?","Clarify the limit and route it to the appropriate responsible person.","Accept silently and improvise.","Ignore it without communicating."],["You cannot meet an agreed timing. What helps?","Communicate early and agree a safe alternative or escalation.","Wait until someone notices.","Mark it complete to avoid concern."],["At transition, an item is unfinished. What next?","Make its status and receiving owner explicit.","Assume the next team will find it.","Delete it from the discussion."]],"time":8,"refs":["team","research-handoff"],"editorial":true,"question":"A task is beyond your authority. What is accountable?","answers":["Accept silently and improvise.","Clarify the limit and route it to the appropriate responsible person.","Ignore it without communicating."],"correct":1,"rationales":["This leaves the key issue unresolved. State what you can do within your role and what needs another person’s authority or expertise. Do not accept a task by implication when responsibility is unclear.","This applies the principle: State what you can do within your role and what needs another person’s authority or expertise. Do not accept a task by implication when responsibility is unclear.","This leaves the key issue unresolved. State what you can do within your role and what needs another person’s authority or expertise. Do not accept a task by implication when responsibility is unclear."],"feedback":"This applies the principle: State what you can do within your role and what needs another person’s authority or expertise. Do not accept a task by implication when responsibility is unclear."},{"id":"compassion-boundaries","cat":"attitudes","title":"Care with compassion and boundaries","tag":"Sustainable compassion","image":"topic-compassion-boundaries","ref":"work","evidence":"wellbeing","tool":"pause","description":"Acknowledge distress while offering realistic support within your role.","steps":[["Notice and acknowledge","Ask what is difficult and listen without judgment. Avoid promises that are outside your control or assumptions about what another person needs."],["Offer a bounded response","Explain what you can help with and what needs a different service or professional. Clear boundaries can make support more reliable."],["Include the working conditions","For persistent strain, use appropriate supervisory, occupational-health or qualified support. A short personal reset does not replace workload review or organisational responsibility."]],"outcome":"Offer an empathetic response with a realistic next step and boundary.","takeaway":"Compassion can be warm, practical and honest about limits.","reflect":"Where would a clearer boundary make your support more dependable?","example":["A fictional colleague is struggling with continuing workload.","A quick breathing exercise will fix it.","I can listen and help identify support. We should also raise the workload concern through the appropriate route.","Identify one realistic support action and one limit."],"checks":[["Which response is compassionate and realistic?","I can help with this step and connect you with the right support.","I promise everything will be fine.","You just need to be stronger."],["What does a short reset address?","A possible momentary pause, not persistent workload problems.","Every cause of workplace distress.","The need for organisational support."],["A support need exceeds your role. What helps?","Acknowledge it and connect to appropriate qualified support.","Promise unlimited availability.","Try an unfamiliar intervention without support."]],"time":8,"refs":["work","research-wellbeing"],"editorial":true,"question":"Which response is compassionate and realistic?","answers":["I promise everything will be fine.","I can help with this step and connect you with the right support.","You just need to be stronger."],"correct":1,"rationales":["This leaves the key issue unresolved. Ask what is difficult and listen without judgment. Avoid promises that are outside your control or assumptions about what another person needs.","This applies the principle: Ask what is difficult and listen without judgment. Avoid promises that are outside your control or assumptions about what another person needs.","This leaves the key issue unresolved. Ask what is difficult and listen without judgment. Avoid promises that are outside your control or assumptions about what another person needs."],"feedback":"This applies the principle: Ask what is difficult and listen without judgment. Avoid promises that are outside your control or assumptions about what another person needs."},{"id":"inclusive-leadership","cat":"attitudes","title":"Invite the voice you have not heard","tag":"Inclusive teamwork","image":"topic-inclusive-leadership","ref":"disability","evidence":"access","tool":"huddle","description":"Make participation possible across roles, communication needs and power differences.","steps":[["Notice who is missing","Ask which roles and perspectives are absent from a decision. Consider who cannot attend, speak easily or access the materials."],["Create more than one route","Offer accessible materials and appropriate ways to contribute before, during or after the discussion. Invite concerns without putting someone on display."],["Show what changed","Acknowledge contributions, explain how they influenced the decision and assign follow-up. An invitation without a response can leave people unheard."]],"outcome":"Adapt a team discussion to include a missing perspective.","takeaway":"An invitation matters more when people can see how it was used.","reflect":"Whose perspective should shape your next improvement discussion?","example":["A fictional meeting repeatedly hears only its most senior members.","Silence means everyone agrees.","Which perspective have we not heard, and what other way to contribute would help?","Offer a second accessible route for input."],"checks":[["A colleague is silent in a meeting. What can you infer?","Their view is unknown; create an appropriate way to contribute.","They agree with every decision.","They have nothing useful to add."],["Which meeting change supports inclusion?","Accessible materials and more than one route to contribute.","Requiring everyone to speak without preparation.","Using only the fastest discussion format."],["After gathering input, what closes the loop?","Explain the decision and how contributions shaped it.","File the comments without response.","Count comments as proof of agreement."]],"time":8,"refs":["disability","research-access"],"editorial":true,"question":"A colleague is silent in a meeting. What can you infer?","answers":["They agree with every decision.","Their view is unknown; create an appropriate way to contribute.","They have nothing useful to add."],"correct":1,"rationales":["This leaves the key issue unresolved. Ask which roles and perspectives are absent from a decision. Consider who cannot attend, speak easily or access the materials.","This applies the principle: Ask which roles and perspectives are absent from a decision. Consider who cannot attend, speak easily or access the materials.","This leaves the key issue unresolved. Ask which roles and perspectives are absent from a decision. Consider who cannot attend, speak easily or access the materials."],"feedback":"This applies the principle: Ask which roles and perspectives are absent from a decision. Consider who cannot attend, speak easily or access the materials."}];
const cases=[{"id":"handover","title":"The handover that almost missed it","cat":"Teamwork","time":4,"body":"During a simulated shift handover, a colleague describes a patient as “stable.” You notice newly documented deterioration and the review plan is unclear. You are concerned.","question":"What is your best first response?","answers":["Finish handover and check again later.","Clearly raise the change, request prompt clinical review, and follow the local escalation pathway.","Update the record but assume someone else will act."],"correct":1,"feedback":"A concern about deterioration needs timely action within your role. State the observed change and urgency; use the local emergency response when indicated. Documentation does not replace escalation.","next":"The receiving clinician acknowledges your concern. What closes the loop?","nextAnswers":["Assume the message was enough.","Confirm who will review, when, and what to do if the patient worsens or help does not arrive.","Repeat every detail from the record."],"nextCorrect":1,"nextFeedback":"Shared understanding includes responsibility, timing, and a contingency plan. The handover is not complete until the next action is clear.","lesson":"sbar"},{"id":"teachback","title":"“Yes, I understand.” But do they?","cat":"Communication","time":3,"body":"In a simulated discharge conversation, a patient agrees with every explanation but cannot describe the follow-up plan. You have limited time.","question":"Which approach best supports understanding?","answers":["Repeat the same explanation more loudly.","Ask a yes/no question one final time.","Use plain language, explain one part at a time, and invite teach-back without blame."],"correct":2,"feedback":"Teach-back checks your explanation. Rephrase in smaller chunks and support language or accessibility needs.","next":"The patient explains part of the plan incorrectly. What next?","nextAnswers":["Thank them, clarify the unclear part in a different way, then check again.","Record “non-compliant.”","Give a leaflet without discussion."],"nextCorrect":0,"nextFeedback":"A misunderstanding is information for the practitioner. Re-explain, check again, and involve the appropriate support rather than labeling the patient.","lesson":"communication"},{"id":"ai-case","title":"The confident answer that needs a check","cat":"Digital responsibility","time":4,"body":"In a fictional training exercise, an AI tool produces a polished summary with a clinical claim and a reference you do not recognize.","question":"Before using the claim, what should happen?","answers":["Verify the actual reference and claim with authoritative sources and qualified review.","Trust it because there is a citation.","Ask the tool whether it is certain and accept “yes.”"],"correct":0,"feedback":"AI may generate inaccurate claims or nonexistent references. A citation is a starting point for checking, not proof.","next":"A colleague wants to paste an identifiable patient note into an unapproved public tool. What should you advise?","nextAnswers":["Remove the name; that always guarantees anonymity.","Do not share the note; use only approved workflows that meet organizational privacy and governance requirements.","Proceed if the tool is free."],"nextCorrect":1,"nextFeedback":"Names are not the only identifiers. Confidential clinical information needs an approved, governed workflow, not an improvised assumption of anonymity.","lesson":"ai"},{"id":"med-case","title":"Two lists. One important pause.","cat":"Patient safety","time":4,"body":"In a simulated transfer, a medication appears on one list but not another. The reason for the difference is not documented.","question":"What is the safest next step?","answers":["Use whichever list was printed last.","Pause and clarify the discrepancy with the responsible clinician or pharmacist.","Combine both lists."],"correct":1,"feedback":"An unexplained discrepancy needs reconciliation. Do not independently infer a prescribing intention or combine lists.","next":"The discrepancy is clarified. What supports a safer transition?","nextAnswers":["Update the appropriate record, communicate the agreed plan, and check the patient’s understanding within your role.","Keep the clarification only in memory.","Leave both lists unchanged."],"nextCorrect":0,"nextFeedback":"A resolution should reach the record, the next team, and the patient through the approved workflow. Clear communication prevents the discrepancy from recurring.","lesson":"safety"}];
const quotes=["Better care begins with a better question.","The smallest pause can make space for the most important thing.","Knowledge informs care. Kindness makes it human.","Clarity is a form of care.","Make the safer action easier to repeat.","A concern voiced early gives the team time to respond.","One useful insight becomes stronger when you try it."];
const assessmentQuestions=[["knowledge","How confident do you feel evaluating evidence and recognizing safety risks?"],["skills","How confident do you feel communicating clearly and coordinating care?"],["attitudes","How consistently can you bring curiosity, empathy, and reflection into practice?"]];
