const cusScenes=[{"title":"Name the concern","event":"In a fictional handover, a planned review has no named owner. The receiving colleague says, “It will probably be sorted.”","good":1,"options":["Ask whether the colleague thinks there is anything to worry about.","State the missing ownership and why you are concerned.","Assume the request was understood and add a note for later."],"why":["An open question can help, but the observed concern still needs to be made explicit.","The message names an observable gap and connects it to the concern.","A note does not resolve responsibility for the planned action."]},{"title":"Make the uncertainty explicit","event":"Your concern has not been resolved. The colleague acknowledges the message but still does not know who owns the review.","good":2,"options":["Repeat that a review was mentioned during the earlier handover.","Explain that you will ask about the review at the next routine meeting.","Say you are uncomfortable proceeding while responsibility is unclear."],"why":["Repeating the history may leave the unresolved responsibility unchanged.","A meeting later is not a substitute for resolving a current concern through the proper route.","This makes the unresolved uncertainty explicit and invites a response."]},{"title":"Connect the concern to safety","event":"Responsibility is still unclear. The fictional team needs an explicit response through its approved process.","good":0,"options":["State the safety concern, request a clear response, and use local escalation if unresolved.","Use more forceful wording about the colleague’s attitude so the issue gets attention.","Stop communicating because the concern has already been raised twice."],"why":["Link the concern to safety and use the appropriate local route. Urgent concerns must be escalated immediately.","Personal accusations distract from the observable risk and the required action.","An unresolved concern needs the appropriate response, not an arbitrary limit on communicating."]}];
